  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nCurrently have a compact upper low spinning over the TX Panhandle \nand about to move into western OK. Widespread showers continue \nacross central and western KS with some of this making it into \nsouthwest and south central KS, mainly just west of our forecast\narea. \n\nUpper low will slowly sink southeast today with the higher rainfall \nchances also pushing southeast. So current thinking is that \nsouthwest portions of our forecast area will have the best chance to \nsee some light rainfall, mainly this morning, with Wichita on the \nnortheast edge of the rainfall. Any location that sees rainfall\ncan expect less than a tenth of an inch. \n\nBy Mon morning, upper low will be situated near the Arklatex region, \nwith upper ridging moving into the Plains. Meanwhile out west, \nanother upper trough will be about to move onshore over CA. This \nwill allow for dry conditions and seasonal temperatures to start the \nwork week. By Tue evening, shortwave trough will be quickly lifting \nout of the Central Great Basin and out across the Central Plains on \nWed. The main affect this system will have will be to bring an \nincrease in south/southwest winds for Tue and especially Wed. On \nTue, it looks like the strongest winds will be mainly west of I-135, \nwith stronger winds expected area wide on Wed afternoon. For both \ndays, the strongest wind gusts are anticipated to be in the 40 to 45 \nmph range. Pattern will remain very progressive with yet another \nshortwave trough expected to come onshore over CA by Thu evening and \nmove out across the Southern/Central Rockies on Fri. Strong 850-\n700mb theta-e advection ahead of the wave will bring increased \nrainfall chances starting Fri morning and continuing through Sat \nwhen the shortwave energy swings across the area. \n\nAs far as temperatures go, warming trend will continue next week \nwith widespread highs in the 60s by Wed with some 70s even \npossible by Fri. A significant cool-down does not look likely \nover the next several days.",
